<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gingee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Senji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gingee, also called Senji, is a town in Viluppuram district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known for its great fortress spread over three granite hills. The fort was the seat of the Gingee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> from the early sixteenth century and later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, whose king Rajaram held it against a long Mughal siege until 1698, before falling to the French and the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gingee.jpg" alt="Gingee" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KARTY JazZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gingee_Fort_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the Maratha commander Shahaji Bhonsle, conquered much of the southern Karnataka plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking Bangalore in 1638 and the fortress of <b>Gingee</b> in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, the nayaka kingdoms that succeeded it, including Madurai, Thanjavur, <b>Gingee</b>, Ikkeri and the Wodeyars of Mysore, inherited its institutions and claimed its legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1509 one line, traditionally founded by Tubaki Krishnappa Nayaka, was installed at <b>Gingee</b> (Senji), controlling the land between the Palar and Kollidam rivers, including the coast around Pondicherry.</blockquote><a href="/state/gingee-nayaks">Gingee Nayaks</a></li></ul></section>
